1
00:00:00,235 --> 00:00:02,343
Every payment on Monad is public:

2
00:00:02,383 --> 00:00:05,690
who paid, who got paid, and how much.

3
00:00:05,993 --> 00:00:09,042
Kakushi is for wallets and payment apps on Monad that need

4
00:00:09,082 --> 00:00:13,268
private payouts, like payroll, merchants and OTC, without

5
00:00:13,308 --> 00:00:15,500
building the cryptography themselves.

6
00:00:15,658 --> 00:00:17,792
The header reads Monad live:

7
00:00:17,876 --> 00:00:21,249
each block goes from proposed to finalized in about six

8
00:00:21,289 --> 00:00:22,657
tenths of a second.

9
00:00:22,735 --> 00:00:26,596
Stealth send. The app finds the wallet's private address,

10
00:00:26,636 --> 00:00:30,198
and 0.01 MON lands at a one-time address.

11
00:00:30,339 --> 00:00:32,834
The receipt came back in about a second.

12
00:00:32,891 --> 00:00:35,583
Privy handles email and social sign-in.

13
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:38,935
Email needs a one-time code, so for this recording I connect

14
00:00:38,975 --> 00:00:40,250
a browser wallet.

15
00:00:43,068 --> 00:00:47,327
Shield, on Sepolia, where gasless withdrawals are economic.

16
00:00:47,468 --> 00:00:50,636
I deposit 0.001 ETH and keep the note.

17
00:00:54,391 --> 00:00:56,816
Then I withdraw to a brand-new address.

18
00:00:56,899 --> 00:00:59,634
The zero-knowledge proof is made in the browser, and the

19
00:00:59,674 --> 00:01:02,803
relayer pays the gas, so the new address never needed

20
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:03,700
funding.

21
00:01:11,191 --> 00:01:15,206
Bridge, Monad to Sepolia, delivered privately.

22
00:01:15,347 --> 00:01:19,522
Maker 1 quotes, then pays a one-time stealth address on

23
00:01:19,562 --> 00:01:24,410
Sepolia. Makers post margin on Monad, and an unpaid transfer

24
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:26,851
can be slashed with a ZK proof;

25
00:01:26,992 --> 00:01:29,463
that path is tested on a fork.

26
00:01:37,346 --> 00:01:41,904
Now the amount. One USDC becomes an encrypted balance, and

27
00:01:41,944 --> 00:01:44,787
only my signature reveals it, in this tab.

28
00:01:51,458 --> 00:01:54,947
Pay privately sends an encrypted 0.3 to a one-time stealth

29
00:01:54,987 --> 00:01:58,364
address. Neither who nor how much is public.

30
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:08,147
Cash out asks the KMS for a signed decryption of just that

31
00:02:08,187 --> 00:02:12,528
amount. Today, one hosted engine holds the FHE key.

32
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:20,162
Bridge payouts are attested by Kakushi's own runner, using

33
00:02:20,202 --> 00:02:22,693
Chainlink CRE workflow logic;

34
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:24,588
it is not a DON yet.

35
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:27,916
The recipient's inbox scans for these payments with a

36
00:02:27,956 --> 00:02:33,245
viewing key. Monad's RPC returns logs 100 blocks at a time,

37
00:02:33,285 --> 00:02:36,476
so a full-history scan takes many minutes today;

38
00:02:36,559 --> 00:02:39,158
an indexed scan is next.

39
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:43,610
For developers, one React component, KakushiPay, brings all

40
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:45,633
of this to any Monad app.

41
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:50,630
Kakushi. ZK hides who, FHE hides how much, live on Monad

42
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:51,623
today.

